 Attempts are recorded before sending. A crash during a request can result in a repeated delivery or consume an attempt without sending; recipients should deduplicate using `x-relay-delivery`. The five-attempt budget is retained across restarts.
 
-`GET /admin/deliveries` returns an array of delivery metadata; `GET /admin/deliveries/:id` returns one record or `404` if absent. Both expose only `id`, `source`, `target`, `receivedAt` (Unix milliseconds), `status`, and `attempts`. Payload bodies are never included, including in errors. These routes have no built-in authentication; restrict access through your deployment's admin access controls.
+`GET /admin/deliveries?limit=100&cursor=...` returns `{ "deliveries": [...], "nextCursor": "..." }` (null cursor on the last page); `GET /admin/deliveries/:id` returns one record or `404` if absent. Both expose only `id`, `source`, `target`, `receivedAt` (Unix milliseconds), `status`, and `attempts`. Payload bodies are never included, including in errors. These routes have no built-in authentication; restrict access through your deployment's admin access controls.
 
 `GET /admin/deliveries/:id/attempts` returns an array in attempt order containing `attempt` (one-based), `startedAt` and `completedAt` (Unix milliseconds), `statusCode`, and `outcome` (`started`, `retry`, `delivered`, or `failed`). An unfinished attempt has a null completion time and status code; network failures also have a null status code. The start is recorded before sending (and before backoff for inline delivery). No request/response bodies or raw errors are included. Existing deliveries without recorded history return `[]`; unknown IDs return `404` and storage failures return a generic `503`. History persists alongside the attempt count in Postgres, including unfinished attempts after interruption.
 
@@ -33,3 +33,7 @@ DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost/relay SOURCES='{...}' npm start
 ```
 
 Tests use in-memory fakes for the queue and database boundary; `npm test` needs no external services. These tests do not validate SQL against a live Postgres instance.
+
+Admin pagination uses ascending `(received_at, id)` order, with bytewise ID ordering to break timestamp ties. The default limit is 100; valid limits are 1–1000. Omit the cursor for the first page, then pass `nextCursor` unchanged. Malformed pagination returns 400. This changes the list response from an array to a page object. Apply `migrations/004_admin_pagination.sql` to index these queries.
+
+Cursors preserve database timestamp precision, including backfilled receipts. Pages use a strict seek rather than offsets: concurrent inserts before the cursor do not shift subsequent pages, and are visible when starting a fresh traversal. Inserts after it can appear in subsequent pages. This is not a snapshot; receipt timestamps and IDs should remain immutable during traversal. Legacy attempt history remains optional.
